   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to policies that allow students with disabilities to participate in a high school graduation ceremony",
    "basePrintNo": "S4729",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4729-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §4402, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S4729\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\npolicies that allow students with disabilities to participate in a\nhigh school graduation ceremony\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nRequires a local policy be put in place to allow students with\ndisabilities to participate in a high school graduation ceremony.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: Short Title-\"Zachary's Law\"\n\nSection 2 amends the education law by requiring the board of education\nor the board of trustees of each school district to establish a policy\nto allow any students with a disability to participate in the\ngraduation ceremony of the student's high school graduating class if\nthe student has been issued a skills and achievement commencement\ncredential but has not otherwise qualified for a regents or local high\nschool diploma. The school district must provide annual written notice\nto all students with disabilities and their parents or guardians about\nthe school district's policy.\n\nSection 3: Effective Date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nCurrently in New York there is no required policy by the State\nEducation Department to allow students with disabilities to\nparticipate in their graduation ceremony. This legislation recognizes\nthat a small number of students with disabilities will need special\nconsiderations while maintaining their academic studies toward the\nhigh school diploma or state equivalent. Some students may need to\nconsider that earning a high school diploma will take longer than four\nyears. Parents and students must have a clear understanding of the\nrequirements to participate in the graduation ceremony, so that the\nstudent and parent can plan on how she or he will meet those\nrequirements.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to state.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\n90 days after enactment.\n",
